          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The term
            %em convenient category of topological spaces
            is used (going back to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Steenrod67"} Steenrod 1967
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+topological+space"} nice
            enough to address many of the needs of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} working topologists
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+categories+of+spaces"} nice categories of spaces
          %p
            A primary example is the category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+spaces"} compactly generated spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-topological+spaces"} D-topological spaces
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            While the authors of this article don’t know whether there exists in the literature a widely accepted definition of “convenient category of topological spaces”, we propose the following definition as reasonable and convenient (see also the discussion
            %a{:href => "#NvC"} below
            on the distinction between “nice” and “convenient”):
          %p
          #ConvenientCategory.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong convenient category of topological spaces
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/replete+subcategory"} replete subcategory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              of the category of all topological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              such that the following conditions 1-3 hold:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW+complex"} CW complex
                  is an object of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi C
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  is
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
            %p
          %p
            Frequently it is also felt desirable to add closure under certain types of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspaces"} subspaces
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is closed under
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspaces"} closed subspaces
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              belongs to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \subseteq X
              is a closed subspace (in
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Top
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              also belongs to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            At times one might hope that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is closed under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subspaces"} open subspaces
            as well, but this does not hold for all objects in some of the well-known examples of convenient categories.
          %p
            It may be well to note that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            need not agree with the corresponding colimits and limits in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a full subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            which contains all CW complexes, the usual sorts of colimits used to present CW complexes are the same whether interpreted in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            or in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is closed under closed subspaces, then an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equalizer"} equalizer
            of a pair of maps between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+spaces"} Hausdorff spaces
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            (being a closed subspace) is the same whether computed in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            or in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            On the other hand,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            of
            = succeed "-objects" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            need not land in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            and the product in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            do not agree. This is in particular the case for compactly generated spaces. In fact, the “compactly generated product” is sometimes preferable to the
            = succeed "-product" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are CW complexes, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times Y
            need not be a CW complex in the usual product topology, but it is in the compactly generated topology.
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#CategoriesOfColimitsOfGeneratingSpaces Categories of colimits of generating spaces
          %p The original example of a convenient category of topological spaces is that of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+space"} compactly generated topological space
          %p and another example is that of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Delta-generated+spaces"} Delta-generated spaces
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean-generated+spaces"}> Euclidean-generated spaces
              ).
          %p
            Both of these are special cases of the general class of convenient
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi GenSp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GenSp
                \-generated topological spaces
          %p
            These are those topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which agree with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            (
            %a{:href => "Top#UniversalConstructions"}> in TopSp
            ) of all continuous images inside them of
            %em generating spaces
            in a specified
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GenSp
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GenSp \,\subset\,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TopSp"} TopSp
            (
            %a{:href => "#Vogt71"}> Vogt 1971, Sec. 1
            ):
          #eq:Gfication.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi T
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi GenSp
                        %mrow
                          %mrow
                            %mi T
                            %mo →
                            %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≃
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G(X)
                  \\,\coloneqq\,
                  \\Big(
                  \\underset{
                  \\underset{
                  { T \in GenSp }
                  \\atop
                  {T \to X}
                  }{\longrightarrow}
                  }{\lim}
                  \\,
                  T
                  \\Big)
                  \\xrightarrow{\;\;\; \simeq \;\;\;}
                  X
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategories"} full subcategories
            of such
            = succeed "-generated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GenSp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GenSp
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective
            (
            %a{:href => "#Vogt71"}> Vogt 1971, Cor. 1.4
            )
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TopSp
                  %munderover
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi G
                    %mo ↩
                  %mi G
                  %mi TopSp
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  TopSp
                  \\underoverset
                  {\underset{G}{\longrightarrow}}
                  {\hookleftarrow}
                  {\;\;\;\;\bot\;\;\;\;}
                  G TopSp
                  \\,.
          %p
            If one demands of the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GenSp
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi TopSp
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GenSp \,\hookrightarrow TopSp\,
            of generating spaces, for any pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi GenSp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1, G_2 \,\in\, GenSp
            that (
            %a{:href => "#Vogt71"}> Vogt 1971, Axiom 2
            ):
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+space"} product topological space
                is again in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi GenSp
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GenSp
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact-open+topology"} compact-open topology
                on the set of maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1 \to G_2
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation+map"} evaluation map
          %p
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mi TopSp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G TopSp
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+closed+category"} Cartesian closed category
            (
            %a{:href => "#Vogt71"}> Vogt 1971, Thm. 3.6
            ), with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            given by the image under
            = succeed "-fication" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Gfication"} (1)
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+open+topology"} compact open topology
          %p
            If one in addition demands that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GenSp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GenSp
            contains all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+simplices"} topological simplices
            (or all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(X) \to X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            (
            %a{:href => "#Vogt71"}> Vogt 1971, Prop. 1.2 (g)
            ).
          %p As special cases of this general construction, the above examples of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+spaces"} compactly generated topological spaces
                is obtained by
              %p
                taking
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi GenSp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GenSp
                to be given by all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+spaces"} compact Hausdorff spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Delta-generated+topological+spaces"} Delta-generated topological spaces
                is obtained by
              %p
                taking
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi GenSp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GenSp
                to be given by all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
                (equivalently by all
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+simplices"} topological simplices
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Gaucher07"} Gaucher 2007, Sec. 2
          %p
            Further developments along these lines are in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#EscardoLawsonSimpson04"} Escardo, Lawson &amp; Simpson 2004
          %p
            The topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            for which there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+law+for+spaces"} exponentiable space
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            (i.e such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Top
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times (-)  \;\colon\; Top \to Top
            has a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %em core-compact
            spaces (spaces whose topology is a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+lattice"} continuous lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            of core-compact spaces, with the property that the product of any two spaces in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            of spaces in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong productive
            = succeed "-colimits" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            are called
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %strong generated
          %p
          #ProductiveClassOfGenerators.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              Say that a class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              is
              %em productive
              if
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  every
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in \mathcal{C}
                  is
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiable+object"} exponentiable
              %li
                %p
                  for every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair"} pair
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1, U_2 \in \mathcal{C}
                  their
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+space"} product space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_1 \times U_2
                  is
                  = succeed "-generated." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %p
          %p
          #ProductiveClassOfGeneratorsImpliesConvenience.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a productive class of generating objects (Def.
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ProductiveClassOfGenerators"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
              whose objects are
              = succeed "-generated" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coreflective+subcategory"} coreflective
                  in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"}> cartesian closed
                  , with the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
                  given by the coreflection of the usual
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
                  with its
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact-open+topology"} compact-open topology
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              The existence of the internal hom is
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#EscardoLawsonSimpson04"} Escardo, Lawson &amp; Simpson 2004 Thm. 5.15
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  find the notation “
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(-)
                  ” for the coreflector on on p. 6,
              %li
                %p
                  observe that the ordinary mapping space with its
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact-open+topology"} compact-open topology
                  is denoted “
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi X
                        %msub
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mo ≪
                              %mi 𝒞
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X \Rightarrow_{\ll_{\mathcal{C}}} Y]
                  ” in Rem. 5.20,
              %li
                %p
                  while the internal hom in
                  = succeed "-generated" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                  = succeed "\u201D" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi X
                          %msub
                            %mo ⇒
                            %mi 𝒞
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo =
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi Map
                            %mi 𝒞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X \Rightarrow_{\mathcal{C}} Y] = \mathcal{C}\big(Map_{\mathcal{C}}[X,Y]\big)
              %li
                %p
                  where “
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Map
                          %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map_{\mathcal{C}}
                  ” is defined in Lemma 3.8,
              %li
                %p
                  and finally the statement
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Map
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi X
                        %msub
                          %mo ⇒
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mo ≪
                              %mi 𝒞
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map_{\mathcal{C}}[X,Y] = [X \Rightarrow_{\ll_{\mathcal{C}}} Y]
                  is Prop. 5.21 on p. 21,
            %p which implies the claim.
          %p
            The other convenience conditions of Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ConvenientCategory"}
            (inclusion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complexes"} CW-complexes
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subspaces"} closed subspaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            are
            = succeed "-generated," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "-generated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed "-generated." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is
            = succeed "-generated," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p Moreover:
          %p
          #RetractsOfCGeneratedSpacesAreCGenerated.num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              The category of
              = succeed "-generated" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retracts"} retracts
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be
              = succeed "-generated" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            #eq:ARetract.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi i
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi r
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    id
                    \\;\colon\;
                    A \xrightarrow{i} X \xrightarrow{r} A
            %p
              a retract, for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"} continuous maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset A
              such that for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \in \mathcal{C}
              and all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+maps"} continuous maps
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \colon C \to A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^{-1}(U) \subset C
              is open in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is already open in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %p
              First note that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r^{-1}(U)
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset A
              is open in
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \in \mathcal{C}
              and continuous maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \colon C \to X
              we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi q
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi q
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q^{-1}\big(r^{-1}(U)\big) = (r \circ q)^{-1}(U)
              is open in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              by assumption on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r^{-1}(U)
              is open in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              by the assumption that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              = succeed "-generated." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %p
              But then the retract property
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ARetract"} (2)
              implies that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi id
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    U
                    \=
                    id^{-1}(U)
                    \=
                    i^{-1}\big(r^{-1}(U)\big)
            %p
              is the preimage of the open subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi r
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r^{-1}(U)
              under the continuous map
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %h3#ExamplesSubcategoriesOfToposes Subcategories of toposes
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#CategoriesOfColimitsOfGeneratingSpaces"} above
            example of the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Delta-generated+topological+spaces"} Delta-generated topological spaces
            %a{:href => "#CategoriesOfColimitsOfGeneratingSpaces"} above
            has the remarkable property that it is
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-topos"} quasi-topos
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
                (see
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a{:href => "diffeological+space#RelationToTopologicalSpaces"} there
            %li
              %p
                which is in turn a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
                (see
                = succeed ");" do
                  %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets#DiffeologicalSpacesAreTheConcreteSmoothSets"} there
            %li
              %p
                which in turn is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+sub-%28infinity%2C1%29-category"}
                  full sub-
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                  \-category
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}
                  cohesive
                  = succeed "-topos" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoids"}
                  smooth
                  = succeed "-groupoids" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            such that the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape modality
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+via+cohesive+path+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} smooth path ∞-groupoid construction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            of topological spaces (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Proper+Orbifold+Cohomology"}> SS20, Ex. 3.18
            , see also at
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+Delta-generated+topological+spaces"} model structure on Delta-generated topological spaces
          %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/TopologicalConvenienceBySmoothGroupoids210930.jpg", :width => "800px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/schreiber/show/Equivariant+principal+infinity-bundles"} SS21
          %h2#counterexamples Counterexamples
          %p
            A naive approach to the problem of constructing “convenient categories” usually runs into problems. For example, one could try to work with the full subcategory of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            consisting of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponentiable+spaces"} exponentiable spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are exponentiable, the exponential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            may not be:
          %ul
            %li
              The category of exponentiable spaces is not cartesian closed. (It is however
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
          %p
            To see this, we recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff space
            is exponentiable if and only if it is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+space"} locally compact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            (provided it exists) is Hausdorff if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            is. Thus, it is enough to exhibit two locally compact Hausdorff spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            whose exponential is not locally compact.
          %p
            Take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \mathbb{R}
            with its usual topology and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = \mathbb{N}
            (the set of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\mathbb{N}
            exists in the category of locally compact Hausdorff spaces. Then it must be a countable product of copies of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            by the following calculation:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi LCH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi LCH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mi LCH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LCH(X \times \mathbb{N}, \mathbb{R}) \cong LCH(\sum_{\mathbb{N}} X, \mathbb{R}) \cong \prod_{\mathbb{N}} LCH(X, \mathbb{R})
          %p
            where the last functor in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mi ℕ
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{\mathbb{N}} \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}} \to \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}}
          %p
            rendering
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}}
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            (a TVS) over the real numbers. But it is well-known that locally compact TVS are finite-dimensional, and we have reached an absurdity.
          %p
            Similarly, the topological product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}
            is homeomorphic to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Baire+space+of+irrational+numbers"} Baire space
          %h2#NvC “Nice” versus “convenient” categories of spaces
          %p
            A related entry is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+category+of+spaces"} nice category of spaces
          %p
            “A convenient category of topological spaces” is the title of
            %a{:href => "#Steenrod"} a well-known paper
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Norman+Steenrod"} Norman Steenrod
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+space"} function space
            constructions (i.e., cartesian closure) as a great convenience for working algebraic topologists, on top of (generally less problematic) completeness and cocompleteness assumptions. (See also the historical remarks that follow.)
          %p
            It is well-known that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            is not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+law+for+spaces"} exponentiable spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+compact+space"} locally compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+space"} Hausdorff
            spaces, but as we saw above, the naive idea of simply cutting down to some of these does not give a good cartesian closed category either, since firstly it need not be complete and cocomplete, and secondly even if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are exponentiable, the exponential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Y
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^X
            need not be.
          %p
            However, one can cut down to
            %em some
            full subcategory of spaces which does admit function spaces. This typically involves the subtle and delicate interplay between compactness conditions and openness conditions. It comes at a cost – that limits and/or colimits in the subcategory might not be computed as they are in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            – but this is generally considered a very small price to pay in exchange for the great convenience of these assumptions.
          %p
            That a convenient category of topological spaces contains all the CW complexes was not explicitly declared by Steenrod, but we feel certain that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topologists
            want these as part of their convenient category. In practice this is a mild assumption, because the usual examples certainly contain all finite topological products of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            = succeed "-colimits" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^n \cong D^n
          %p “Nice categories of spaces” should be thought of as a wider and vaguer term; it really means the category of spaces has “nice” categorical properties for some mathematical purpose at hand. Certainly any convenient category of spaces should be considered a nice category of spaces for the general purposes of algebraic topology. On the other hand, there are categories of spaces which are not “convenient” in the technical sense described above, but which may be very nice for certain purposes.
          %p
            For example, the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+spaces"} compact Hausdorff spaces
            can be considered as being very nice for certain purposes. For instance, it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+category"} monadic
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            (!) and a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equilogical+spaces"} equilogical spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
          %p
            It should also be noted that “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"}> space
            ” itself has a wider meaning than the technical notion of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"}> topological space
            ”, even if topological intuitions come into play. For example, in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+theory"} domain theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            (for example,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed-complete+partial+order"} dcpos
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott-continuous+map"} Scott-continuous map
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            is in some respects “nice”, but these are not topological spaces either.
          %p
            Along with the entry on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+category+of+spaces"} nice category of spaces
            and the examples above, see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Johnstone%27s+topological+topos"} Johnstone's topological topos
            and Spanier’s
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasitopological+space"} quasitopological space
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Spanier"} ref
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "##Booth71"} #Booth 1971
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "##CrabbJames98"} #Crabb &amp; James 1998
          %p
            There are possible advantages in homotopy theory of using a “topological topos” (
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone"}> Johnstone
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Harasani"} Harasani
          %h2#HistoricalRemarks Historical remarks
          %p
            The phrase “convenient category of topological spaces” predates
            %a{:href => "#Steenrod67"} Steenrod 1967
            by a number of years;
            %a{:href => "#Brown63"} Brown 1963, Introduction
            says:
          %blockquote
            %p
              It may be that the category of Hausdorff
              = succeed "-spaces" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            The requirements for convenience were spelled out in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Brown64"} Brown 1964
          %p
            In fact, Brown (not Steenrod, as has sometimes been assumed) was the first to prove that Hausdorff
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kelley+space"} k-spaces
            (with the “kelleyfied” weakening of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact-open+topology"} compact–open topology
            on function spaces) formed what is now called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Brown61"} Brown 1961
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Cohen54"} Cohen 1954
          %p
            Note Brown’s work predates the formal introduction of cartesian closed categories in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} monoidal closed
            and cartesian closed categories, which was to attract much attention throughout the sixties. Further, Brown’s results on the topological case are more precise than Steenrod’s, since the 1964 paper deals with functions continuous on compact subsets, and for these obtains a homeomorphism without kellyfication. An account of this may be found in the book
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Brown06"} Topology and Groupoids
          %p
            Of course, as has often been emphasized by Lawvere, the need for and convenience of considering function spaces is a very old idea in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            (going back to the roots of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/calculus+of+variations"} calculus of variations
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Kelley"} John Kelley
            %em General Topology
            (1955). The relevance of what are today called
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kelley+space"} Kelley space
            = succeed ")" do
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} completeness
            of function spaces (wrt the compact–open topology) when the base is a complete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+space"} uniform space
            and the exponent is a k-space. However, the problem of obtaining a class of spaces
            %em closed
            under function spaces wasn’t solved prior to Brown’s thesis. Brown also observed the relevance of k-spaces to studies of how products interact with
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient space
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunctions"} adjunctions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \times X
            preserves colimits if it has an exponential right adjoint
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^Y
            when the function spaces have the compact-open topology, and so to give a monoidal closed structure on the category of Hausdorff spaces; the generalisation to the non-Hausdorff case is given in the paper of Booth and Tillotson listed below.
          %p
            Appreciation of the role of convenient categories was in full force by the early seventies (for a sample, see
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
            = succeed "," do
              %em Geometry of Iterated Loop Spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#KM"} Kriegl and Michor
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+space"} compactly generated topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Delta-generated+topological+space"}> Delta-generated topological space
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-topological+space"} D-topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-topological+space"} quasi-topological space
          %h2#references References
          %p The terminology (“convenient category of topological spaces”) became widely adopted with
          %ul
            %li#Steenrod67
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Norman+Steenrod"}> Norman Steenrod
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em A convenient category of topological spaces
              %strong 14
              (1967) 133–152 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1307/mmj/1028999711"}> doi:10.1307/mmj/1028999711
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.mmj/1028999711"} euclid:mmj/1028999711
          %p
            referring there to the category of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+space"} compactly generated topological space
            (see the references
            %a{:href => "compactly+generated+topological+space#Refererences"} there
            for more), whose convenient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closure
            had
            %a{:href => "#HistoricalRemarks"} previously
            been highlighted in:
          %ul
            %li#Brown61
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Some problems of algebraic topology: a study of function spaces, function complexes, and FD-complexes
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:3af55800-4be7-462f-b91d-9769a6dac2c4"} uuid:3af55800-4be7-462f-b91d-9769a6dac2c4
              %blockquote
                %p (NB: the Appendix of this thesis was withdrawn from the examination.)
            %li#Brown63
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Ten topologies for
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                          %mo ×
                          %mi Y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times Y
                %strong 14
                2 (1963) 303–319 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/qmath/14.1.303"}> doi:10.1093/qmath/14.1.303
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://groupoids.org.uk/pdffiles/tentopologies.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Brown64
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Function spaces and product topologies
                %strong 15
                (1964) 238–250. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/qmath/15.1.238"}> doi:10.1093/qmath/15.1.238
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p influenced, in turn, by:
          %ul
            %li#Cohen54
              D. E. Cohen:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Spaces with weak topology
              %strong 5
              2 (1954) 77–80 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/qmath/5.1.77"}> doi:10.1093/qmath/5.1.77
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Discussion in the generality that subsumes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+generated+topological+spaces"} compactly generated topological spaces
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Delta-generated+topological+spaces"} Delta-generated topological spaces
            and all cases of subcategory-generated spaces in between:
          %ul
            %li#Vogt71
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rainer+M.+Vogt"}> Rainer M. Vogt
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Convenient categories of topological spaces for homotopy theory
                %strong 22
                (1971) 545–555 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01222616"}> doi:10.1007/BF01222616
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Wyler73
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oswald+Wyler"}> Oswald Wyler
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Convenient categories for topology
                %strong 3
                3 (1973) 225-242 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0016-660X(72)90014-1"}> doi:10.1016/0016-660X(72)90014-1
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#EscardoLawsonSimpson04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mart%C3%ADn+Escard%C3%B3"}> Martín Escardó
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jimmie+Lawson"} Jimmie Lawson
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Simpson"} Alex Simpson
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Comparing Cartesian closed categories of (core) compactly generated spaces
                %strong 143
                1–3 (2004) 105-145 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.topol.2004.02.011"}> doi:10.1016/j.topol.2004.02.011
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#FajstrupRosicky08
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lisbeth+Fajstrup"}> Lisbeth Fajstrup
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Rosick%C3%BD"} Jiří Rosický
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A convenient category for directed homotopy
                %strong 21
                1 (2008) 7-20 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.3937"}> arXiv:0708.3937
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/21/1/21-01abs.html"} tac:21-01
            %li#Gaucher07
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philippe+Gaucher"}> Philippe Gaucher
                , Section 2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopical interpretation of globular complex by multipointed d-space
                %strong 22
                22 (2009) 588-621, &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0710.3553"}> arXiv:0710.3553
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p On the abstract construction principle of these examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"}> Michael Barr
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Building closed categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cahiers"} Cahiers Topologie Géométrie Différentielle
              %strong 19
              (1978) 115–129 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=CTGDC_1978__19_2_115_0"}> numdam:CTGDC_1978__19_2_115_0
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Spanier
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edwin+Spanier"}> Edwin Spanier
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quasi-topologies
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1215/s0012-7094-63-03001-1"} doi
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (on
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-topological+spaces"} quasi-topological spaces
            %li#Booth71
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+I.+Booth"}> Peter I. Booth
                \:
                %em The exponential law of maps. II.
                Math. Z.
                %strong 121
                4 (1971) 311–319 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01109977"}> doi:10.1007/bf01109977
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#BT
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+I.+Booth"}> Peter I. Booth
                , J. Tillotson:
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Monoidal closed, Cartesian closed and convenient categories of topological spaces
                %strong 88
                1 (1980) 35–53. &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2140/pjm.1980.88.35"}> doi:10.2140/pjm.1980.88.35
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.pjm/1102779712"} euclid:pjm/1102779712
            %li#CrabbJames98
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Crabb"}> Michael Crabb
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ioan+James"} Ioan James
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fibrewise homotopy theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4471-1265-5"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4471-1265-5
            %li#Preuss02
              %p
                Gerhard Preuß,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundations of topology: an approach to convenient topology
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-010-0489-3"} doi:10.1007/978-94-010-0489-3
          %p survey in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Convenient topology – a new branch of topology
                (
                %a{:href => "http://at.yorku.ca/i/a/a/c/54.htm"}> web
                )
            %li#Brown06
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronald+Brown"}> Ronald Brown
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Spaces of functions and the compact-open topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topology and Groupoids
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://groupoids.org.uk/topgpds.html"} webpage
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://groupoids.org.uk/pdffiles/topgrpds-e.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.sas.rochester.edu/mth/sites/doug-ravenel/otherpapers/rbrown-topgrpds-e.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
          %ul
            %li#Johnstone
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On a topological topos
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/s3-38.2.237"} doi:10.1112/plms/s3-38.2.237
            %li#Harasani
              %p
                Harasani, Hamed A.
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topos theoretic methods in general topology
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://groupoids.org.uk/harasani.html"} pdf
          %p
            Analogous considerations for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            (subsuming
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-topological+spaces"} D-topological spaces
          %ul
            %li#KM
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Kriegl"}> Andreas Kriegl
              ;
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Michor"} Peter Michor
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Convenient+Setting+of+Global+Analysis"} The Convenient Setting of Global Analysis
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 53
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/surv/053"} doi:10.1090/surv/053
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  The ‘
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                  ’ certainly refers to ‘kompakt’ rather than Kelley’s initial.
